window.BIZ_PAPERS = (window.BIZ_PAPERS || []).concat([{"id": "MEDI1-SAM", "unit": 1, "key": "SAM", "series": "Sample assessment (AAQ)", "date": "Pearson sample assessment material", "title": "Pearson sample paper", "source": "pearson", "kind": "exam", "code": "Sample", "spec": "new", "total": 80, "minutes": 90, "items": [{"q": 1, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Identify the type of molecule labelled R in Figure 1.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct response\n• phospholipid", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain why R is not a polymer.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct reason that R is not a polymer\n(1) and one mark for a justification for that response (1), up to\na maximum of two marks\n• It is not made of monomers/repeating units (1)\n• because it consists of three types of molecule (fatty\nacid, phosphate and glycerol) (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "b(i)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Identify the molecule labelled S.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct response\n• cholesterol", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Figure 2 shows an electron micrograph of a bundle of neurones within a section of\nskeletal muscle.\n(Source: © HERVE CONGE, ISM / SCIENCE PHOTO LIBRARY)\nFigure 2\nThe magnification of the image in Figure 2 is × 825.\nThe observed diameter of the bundle of neurones (nerve cells) is 66 mm.\nCalculate the actual diameter, in micrometres (µm), of the bundle of nerve cells.\nShow your working.\nactual diameter = µm", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer:\n• 80\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for knowing how to convert mm to\nmicrometers (µm)\n  - mm x 1000 = µm\n• award one mark for the correct recall of the formula in\nwords or numbers\n  - actual object diameter = observed diameter ÷\nmagnification\n  - object = 66 ÷ 825 (ECF applied for using own\nconversion into micrometres)\n• award one mark for calculating actual diameter using\nown figures of the bundle of nerve cells (ECF)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "Q3", "ctx": ["Figure 3 shows a graph of blood glucose levels after eating bread for a person without diabetes and a person with Type 1 diabetes. Blood glucose level, mg/100ml Diabetes Without diabetes Hours after eating Figure 3 One similarity of the blood glucose levels of the two people shown in Figure 3 is that both levels eventually decrease."], "text": "Explain one other similarity between the blood glucose levels for the two people\nshown in Figure 3.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct similarity between the two blood\nglucose levels (1) and one mark for a justification of that\nsimilarity (1), up to a maximum of two marks.\n• Both blood glucose levels spike after eating (1)\nbecause carbohydrates have been broken down by\nenzymes / amylase into glucose during digestion and\nabsorbed into the bloodstream (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order\nDo not credit generic justifications, e.g., because carbohydrates\nare being broken down into glucose", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "Q3", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain one difference between the blood glucose levels for the two people\nshown in Figure 3.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct difference between the two blood\nglucose levels (1) and one mark for a justification for that\ndifference (1), up to a maximum of two marks\n• Blood glucose level does not decrease as fast for person\nwith type I diabetes (1) because insulin has not been\nreleased from the beta cells of the pancreas to trigger\nabsorption of glucose into the cells (1)\n• Blood glucose levels are always higher for the person\nwith type 1 diabetes (1) because insulin has not been\nreleased from the beta cells of the pancreas to trigger\nabsorption of glucose into the cells (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "Q4", "ctx": [], "text": "Discuss how the different types of neurones involved in the reflex arc are each\nadapted for their function.", "ms": {"body": "Answers will be credited according to the student’s\ndemonstration of knowledge and understanding of the material,\nusing the indicative content and levels descriptors below. The\nindicative content that follows is not prescriptive. Answers may\ncover some or all of the indicative content, but students should\nbe rewarded for other relevant answers.\nSensory (afferent) neurones:\n• carry impulses from receptor into CNS via dorsal root\n• cell body along its length\n• myelin sheath\n• Schwann cells\n• nodes of Ranvier\n• allows saltatory conduction so fast conduction of\nimpulses\n• longer dendron than in motor neurones\n• long axon\n• synapses with relay neurone\n• neurotransmitter released at presynaptic knob\nRelay/intermediate/inter neurones:\n• non myelinated\n• short so impulse does not have to travel far\n• in central part of spinal cord\n• synapse with sensory and motor neurones so have\nreceptors for neurotransmitter released by sensory\nneurones\n• releases vesicles of neurotransmitter at synapse with\nmotor neurone\n• ensure the correct response to a specific stimulus is\nmade\nmotor neurones:\n• cell body at one end\n• long axon and short dendrites\n• myelinated\n• have saltatory conduction\n• carries impulses to effector organ/muscle\n• release neurotransmitter from presynaptic knob at\nneuromuscular junction\nAll three types:\n• organelles in the cell body\n• cytoskeleton moves some to synaptic knob\n• same mode of generation of action potentials/resting\npotential\n• all can become hyperpolarised and have refactory period\nso impulse transmission is one way only\n• reflex actions are protective and response can be made\nwithout involving brain/thought\n33\nLevel  Mark  Descriptor - Discuss 9 marks\nRefer to Levels-Based Mark Scheme Guidance\nGuidance to markers\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as the first trait\n(analysis).\n0 0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1-3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by superficially\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is partially relevant to the context of the question\n2\n4-6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is relevant to the context of the question\n3\n7-9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is consistently relevant to the context of the", "levels": null}}, {"q": 5, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "Q5", "ctx": [], "text": "State two risk factors for developing osteoporosis.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correctly given risk factor up to a\nmaximum of two marks\n• Lack of oestrogen/lack of testosterone\n• Increasing age/being female after menopause\n• Lack of vitamin D\n• Being thin/low body mass/low BMI\n• Smoking tobacco\n• Alcohol consumption\n• Steroid medicines\n• Overactive thyroid gland/hyperthyroidism/Graves disease\n• Long periods of inactivity\nAccept any other appropriate response", "levels": null}}, {"q": 6, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "Q6", "ctx": [], "text": "A peak flow meter is used to measure the maximum rate at which air can be exhaled.\nThis is called peak expiratory flow rate (PEF).\nFigure 4 shows the normal PEF for men.\n15 20 25 30 35 40 45 50 55 60 65 70 75 80 85\nPEF/dm3 min–1\nAge/years\n(Source from: https://www.activ8rlives.com/support/data-collected/)\nFigure 4\nThe PEF values for men with COPD are 45% lower than the normal PEF.\nCalculate the PEF for a 40-year-old man who has COPD.\nShow your working.\nThe PEF for a 40-year-old man with COPD is L minute−1", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer:\n• 357.5 (L minute -1)\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for extracting correct figure from graph\no 650 L minute−1\n• award one mark for correct recall of formula in words or\nnumbers (accept any other appropriate formula)\no Normal PEF × (100% − 45%)\no 650 × 0.55\naward one mark for calculating using own figures the PEF for a\n40-year-old man who has COPD (ECF)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 7, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "Q7", "ctx": [], "text": "Figure 5 shows photomicrographs of human cells in stages of mitosis.\nP\nQ\nR\nS\nFigure 5\nIdentify the correct sequence for mitosis\nA P  Q  R  S\nB Q  S  P  R\nC P  S  R  Q\nD S  Q  R  P", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct sequence\nOption C - P S R Q (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 8, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q8", "ctx": ["Figure 6 shows a human skeleton. Z X Y Figure 6"], "text": "Identify the bones labelled X and Y in Figure 6.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correctly identified bone on the correct\nline up to a maximum of two marks\nX = humerus (1)\nY = pelvic girdle (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 8, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q8", "ctx": [], "text": "Identify the type of joint found in structure Z, in Figure 6.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct response\n• fixed/immoveable (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 8, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "Q8", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain how the synovial membrane helps joints move.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct way that the synovial membrane\nhelps joints move and one mark for a justification for that\nresponse (1), up to a maximum of two marks\n• The synovial membrane reduces friction (1) because it\nsecretes synovial fluid (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order", "levels": null}}, {"q": 9, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "Q9", "ctx": [], "text": "Identify the missing words, 1, 2 and 3, in the flow diagram.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correctly identified missing word in the\ncorrect line up to a maximum of three marks\n1. Calcium ions (1)\n2. Myosin-binding (1)\n3. ATP (adenosine triphosphate) (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 10, "sub": "a", "marks": 4, "section": "Q10", "ctx": ["The snake, Crotalus durissus terrificus, produces a toxin called CVX. CVX is a protein that activates thrombocytes. It may lead to strokes or heart attacks. Figure 8 shows the concentration of thrombin in a sample of blood treated with CVX. Concentration of thrombin in mmol2 0.0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 3.5 4.0 Time / minutes Figure 8"], "text": "Calculate the rate of thrombin production during the last two minutes of the\ninvestigation.\nShow your working", "ms": {"body": "Award four marks for the correct answer:\n• 125 (accept 120-130)\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for extracting the correct for\nconcentration of thrombin at 2 minutes\n  - 400\n• one mark for extracting the correct for concentration of\nthrombin at 4 minutes\n  - 150 (accept 140-160)\n• award one mark for correct recall of formula in words or\nnumbers\n  - Difference in concentration ÷ time\n  - 250 ÷ 2\n• award one mark for calculating rate of thrombin during\nthe last 2 minutes using own figures (ECF)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 10, "sub": "b", "marks": 3, "section": "Q10", "ctx": ["Rate of thrombin production =  mmol L−1 minute−1"], "text": "Describe how CVX may cause seizures, strokes or heart attacks.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each of the following points given in the\ncorrect order, up to a maximum of three marks.\nThrombin catalyses conversion of (soluble blood protein)\nfibrinogen to fibrin (1) so clot forms in blood vessels/named\nblood vessel which travels to brain or heart (1) and leads to\nreduction in oxygen (and glucose) supply to cells for\nrespiration/to make ATP so cells die/cannot function (1)\nAccept any other appropriate response", "levels": null}}, {"q": 11, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "Q11", "ctx": [], "text": "Discuss how proteins are digested and absorbed from the gut into the blood.", "ms": {"body": "Answers will be credited according to the student’s\ndemonstration of knowledge and understanding of the material,\nusing the indicative content and levels descriptors below. The\nindicative content that follows is not prescriptive. Answers may\ncover some or all of the indicative content, but students should\nbe rewarded for other relevant answers.\n• Lumen of gut is outside of body so proteins need to be\nhydrolysed to smaller molecules so they can pass across\ngut wall into blood stream\n• mechanical digestion in the mouth\n• hydrochloric acid in stomach allows for unfolding of\nproteins\n• HCl kills pathogens\n• pepsin adapted to work at low pH\n• HCl activates (inactive) pepsinogen to (active) pepsin\n• pepsin in stomach hydrolyses proteins to peptides\n• mechanical digestion in the stomach aids breakdown of\nprotein molecules\n• trypsin secreted by pancreas into small intestine\n• bile salts from the liver/gall bladder neutralise acidic\nchyme/give PH of about 7.8\n• enterokinase changes trypsinogen to trypsin\n• trypsin hydrolyses peptides to amino acids,\n• large surface area of small intestine for absorption of\namino acids\n• villi structure and microvilli on epithelial cells increase\nsurface area\n• long length and folded inner lining of ileum increase\nsurface area\n• amino acids are absorbed into blood using active\ntransport\n• ATP and carrier proteins are required\n• cotransport\n37\nLevel  Mark  Descriptor - Discuss 9 marks\nRefer to Levels-Based Mark Scheme Guidance\nGuidance to markers\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as the first trait\n(analysis).\n0 0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1-3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by superficially\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is partially relevant to the context of the question\n2\n4-6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is relevant to the context of the question\n3\n7-9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is consistently relevant to the context of the", "levels": null}}, {"q": 12, "sub": "a", "marks": 1, "section": "Q12", "ctx": [], "text": "State the function of the coronary arteries.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct function of the coronary arteries\n• to supply oxygenated blood/oxygen/nutrients to the heart\nmuscle/myocardium (and removes carbon dioxide/heat)(1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 12, "sub": "b", "marks": 1, "section": "Q12", "ctx": [], "text": "State what is meant by myogenic.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct meaning of myogenic\n• (muscle) contracts on its own without stimulation from a\nnerve", "levels": null}}, {"q": 13, "sub": "a", "marks": 1, "section": "Q13", "ctx": ["Muscle cells contain contractile proteins."], "text": "Name the monomers of proteins.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct name of the monomers\n• amino acids", "levels": null}}, {"q": 13, "sub": "b", "marks": 1, "section": "Q13", "ctx": [], "text": "Name the bond that joins two monomers of proteins.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct type of bond\n• peptide (bond)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "Q14", "ctx": [], "text": "State two functions of the kidneys.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each function, up to a maximum of two\nmarks:\n• excretion  (1)\n• osmoregulation (1)\nhelps to regulate blood pressure (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "Q14", "ctx": ["Figure 9 shows the structure of a nephron in the kidney. R Q Figure 9"], "text": "Name structures Q and R in Figure 9.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correct label up to a maximum of two\n• Q = Bowman’s capsule (1)\nR = loop of Henle (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "c", "marks": 1, "section": "Q14", "ctx": ["Q R"], "text": "Name the hormone that regulates the amount of water reabsorbed from the\ncollecting duct.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correctly given hormone\nADH (anti-diuretic hormone)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "d(i)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q14", "ctx": ["There is glucose in the filtrate in the PCT of a nephron but no glucose in the urine", "in the collecting duct."], "text": "State why there is glucose in the filtrate in the PCT of a nephron.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct reason\nGlucose is in the filtrate because it has been forced out of the\nglomerulus (by ultrafiltration) as it is a small molecule (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "d(ii)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q14", "ctx": [], "text": "State why there is no glucose in the urine in the collecting duct.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct reason\nGlucose is not in the urine because it has been reabsorbed from\nthe PCT because it is needed for respiration (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 15, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "Q15", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two ways in which the alveoli are adapted for efficient gas exchange.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correct way in which the alveoli are\nadapted and one mark for a justification for that adaptation, up\nto a maximum of four marks.\n• large total surface area (1) because there are large\nnumber of them (1)\n• large surface area to volume ratio (1) because of the\nspherical shape (1)\n• prevented from collapsing (1) because they have a layer of\nsurfactant (1)\n• maintains steep concentration gradient (1) because of\nblood flow in surrounding network of capillaries (1)\n• very short diffusion distance for gases (1) because the\nwalls of capillaries and alveoli are a single layer of cells\n(squamous epithelium) (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order", "levels": null}}, {"q": 16, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "Q16", "ctx": ["There are different types of muscle tissue found in the body."], "text": "Explain one difference between fast twitch and slow twitch muscle fibres.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct difference between fast twitch and\nslow twitch muscle fibres and one mark for a justification for that\ndifference, up to a maximum of two marks.\n• fast twitch muscle fibres have fewer mitochondria (1)\nbecause they carry out anaerobic respiration (1)\n• Fast twitch muscle contains fewer blood vessels (1)\nbecause they do not need a large supply of oxygenated\nblood (1)\n• Fast twitch muscle fibres contain less myoglobin (1)\nbecause they do not need stored oxygen (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 16, "sub": "b", "marks": 4, "section": "Q16", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two differences between the structure of cardiac and skeletal muscle\ntissue.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct difference between the structure of\ncardiac and skeletal muscle tissue and one mark for a\njustification of that difference, up to a maximum of four\nmarks.\n• Skeletal muscle is multinucleated (1) because they are\nformed from the fusion of (embryonic) myoblasts (1)\n• cardiac muscle  is mononucleate (1) because it consists of\nmany cells joined in series/is a syncytium (1)\n• Faster spread of impulses (1) because cardiac muscle has\nintercalated discs (1)\n• longer lasting action potential (of muscle cell membranes)\n(1) because potassium ions leave more slowly than they\ndo in skeletal muscle (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any order.\nAccept any other appropriate response\nAccept reverse arguments such as:\n• Cardiac muscle is mononucleated (1) because it is not\nformed from the fusion of myoblasts (1).\nDo not credit reverse arguments twice. For example, do not\naward 2 marks if a student gives “Skeletal muscle is\nmultinucleated” and “Cardiac muscle is mononucleated”", "levels": null}}, {"q": 17, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "Q17", "ctx": [], "text": "Neutrophils are white blood cells that ingest bacteria.\nIn glands, hormones are released from cells but do not pass through the cell surface\nmembranes.\nDiscuss the roles of mitochondria, Golgi bodies and the cytoskeleton of cells in the\nbulk transport of substances, such as bacteria and hormones, into and out of cells.", "ms": {"body": "Answers will be credited according to the student’s demonstration\nof knowledge and understanding of the material, using the\nindicative content and levels descriptors below. The indicative\ncontent that follows is not prescriptive. Answers may cover some\nor all of the indicative content, but students should be rewarded\nfor other relevant answers.\n• if large molecules/substances are passing into/out of a\ncell, not through the membrane it is by endo- or exo-\ncytosis\n• endocytosis is into the cell\n• exocytosis is out of the cell\n• both are examples of bulk transport\n• both are active processes and require ATP, so the\nmitochondria are sites of aerobic respiration/named\nstage(s) of aerobic respiration and will produce a lot of ATP\n• vesicles are involved\n• they are moved within the cell/towards the cell membrane\nin the cell by cytoskeleton and motor proteins\n• neutrophils ingest bacteria to kill and digest them\n• hormones are large molecules\n• many are proteins and are modified at Golgi apparatus\nbefore being packaged into vesicles (of cell membrane)\n• move to and fuse with cell surface membrane\n• release contents to outside of cell\nsecreted by specific cells\nLevel  Mark  Descriptor - Discuss 9 marks\nRefer to Levels-Based Mark Scheme Guidance\nGuidance to markers\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as the first trait\n(analysis).\n0 0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1-3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by superficially\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is partially relevant to the context of the question\n2\n4-6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component parts\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and understanding\nthat is relevant to the context of the question\n3\n7-9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is consistently relevant to the context of the\n41\nApril 2026\nFor information about Pearson Qualifications, including Pearson Edexcel\nand BTEC qualifications visit qualifications.pearson.com\nEdexcel and BTEC are registered trademarks of Pearson Education Limited\nPearson Education Limited. Registered in England and Wales No. 872828\nRegistered Office: 80 Strand, London WC2R 0RL.\nVAT Reg No GB 278 537121", "levels": null}}], "badge": "Pearson · current spec", "badgeTone": "new", "notice": "Pearson’s sample assessment for the current AAQ specification: the closest match to the real exam. Figures, tables and information booklets are described in words."}]);
